From a00a1a4d2723504d422abbef9d336b81129d3b14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 03:55:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: SDR targets for Dolby Vision mapped to SDR:
 Reference, Bright room and Projector

A Dolby Vision video on a display that takes no HDR is mapped to SDR for one display
only: white at 203 cd/m2 and the pure 2.4 power of BT.1886. A TV set very bright, or a
projector, is another display, and the picture looks wrong on it without a way to say so.

videoscreen.hadesdvsdrtarget (advanced, Reference by default) says what display the SDR
is made for: Reference is the output as it was, byte for byte; Bright room is a display
that shows white at 300 cd/m2 with gamma 2.2; Projector one that shows white at 100 cd/m2.
The mapping takes the white as the peak the scene is mapped to (BT.2390) and, for Bright
room, the pure 2.2 power as the EOTF of its target (dvbridge_hdr10_mapping::gamma22, of an
SDR target only). Nothing is clipped in any of them.

Checked on Mesa's software OpenGL ES with the target built as map_to_display() builds it:
patches of 1 to 50 cd/m2 get the codes of the display model of each target
((luminance / white) ^ (1 / gamma)) within 0.0034 (Reference, Bright room) and, for the
Projector, up to 10 cd/m2 within 0.0026, with the codes below the model above that, where
its curve compresses. Not seen on a display: what looks right in a room is for the user.

Strings: core 41130 to 41134, Estuary 32610 to 32614.
